Iteration 29: Proposed new text for estimation_template: You are forecasting whether {forecasted_model} will solve a target task on a single attempt under this benchmark's standard evaluation.

Below is a capability profile: tasks from the same benchmark, each labeled with whether {forecasted_model} actually solved it, grouped into comparable-difficulty clusters. Each cluster reports {forecasted_model}'s empirical pass rate on similar tasks.

{capability_profile}

Target task:
{target_task_text}

Produce a single probability that {forecasted_model} solves the target task on one attempt. Follow these steps exactly.

STEP 1 — Identify ALL plausibly-matching clusters.
List every cluster whose tasks resemble the target in source/category (e.g. PicoCTF, CSAW, HackTheBox), format, file/service setup, and apparent complexity. Do NOT prematurely jump to the single hardest-looking cluster. Note the empirical pass rate (successes / total) for each candidate.

STEP 2 — Choose a blended anchor across the matching clusters.
Compute the smoothed rate for each candidate cluster:

    adjusted_rate = (successes + 1.5) / (total + 3)

Then form your anchor as a weighted average of the candidate clusters, weighting by how closely each cluster matches the target (category, source, setup, complexity). When two clusters are both plausible, lean toward the one whose example tasks share the SAME source/category label as the target, but do not ignore the other. This blending prevents the systematic error of over-anchoring to an unrepresentative extreme cluster.

Reference values for the smoothing: 0/11 -> 0.12; 4/11 -> 0.39; 52/52 -> 0.97; 34/40 -> 0.83; 18/40 -> 0.44; 7/40 -> 0.20; 46/52 -> 0.86; 13/18 -> 0.69; 5/18 -> 0.31.

STEP 3 — Weight toward the model's demonstrated overall competence.
Look at the model's pass rates across ALL clusters. If this model solves most tasks in the benchmark (high rates broadly), it likely also handles borderline tasks better than a pessimistic single-cluster read suggests — pull your anchor UP toward the model's stronger clusters. If this model fails most tasks broadly (low rates), pull DOWN. Strong models (broad rates mostly ≥0.7) should rarely be forecast below ~0.40 on any task that resembles a category they sometimes solve; weak models (broad rates mostly ≤0.3) should rarely be forecast above ~0.55 on genuine challenge tasks.

STEP 4 — Adjust modestly for task-specific features (by at most ~0.10 total, and apply sparingly).
Nudge DOWN only for clearly exceptional difficulty: custom binary pwn requiring novel exploitation, heavy cryptographic exploitation, large multi-file source trees, or genuinely vague/missing objectives.
Nudge UP for: single static file, well-specified analysis with a stated solution approach, simple command generation, pattern/syntax fixes, "just run this script" style tasks.
IMPORTANT: Do NOT stack multiple downward nudges. Features like "no files provided," "web challenge," or "blind discovery" are NOT reliable predictors of failure — in practice such tasks are solved about as often as their cluster rate. Treat the empirical cluster rate as the dominant signal; features are a minor correction only.

STEP 5 — Respect these bounds.
- For genuine multi-step challenge tasks (any CTF-style task with a binary, service, exploit, or multi-file setup), keep the forecast within [0.12, 0.90]. Do not go more extreme than this even if a single small cluster looks extreme.
- Reserve values above 0.92 or below 0.08 ONLY for trivially decidable tasks: multiple-choice factual recall, standard-port/protocol questions, one-line syntax fixes, direct command lookups, or "run this provided script" tasks. For these, near-certainty is appropriate.

STEP 6 — Avoid the miscalibration trap.
Binary outcomes punish wishy-washy middle values. Do NOT default to 0.50 or hover near it out of caution. If your blended anchor plus competence weighting points clearly above 0.5, commit above it; if clearly below, commit below it. Report the anchor you actually computed rather than shrinking toward the middle.

STEP 7 — Express uncertainty.
Report p50 as your best estimate. Set p25 below and p75 above it. Widen the interval when the matching clusters are small or disagree; narrow it only for trivially certain tasks. p25 and p75 may equal p50 only for trivially certain tasks.

Keep your rationale to one or two short sentences naming the candidate clusters and their rates, the blended anchor, the competence adjustment, and any feature nudge or bound applied.

